Yes. I would merge those source-report controls into the new analysis rather than replace them. They actually make the dashboard stronger because you now have two different layers:

Source/business control = “Does this breach the threshold already defined by the source report?”
Historical/statistical analysis = “Is the current behavior unusual compared with this UIM's own history?”

Those answer different questions.

For each UIM/field, I would structure the analysis conceptually like this:

Area	Field	Meaning

Current	Records	Current population
Current	DQ Count	Current exceptions
Current	DQ Rate	Current DQ %
Source Control	Threshold Limit	Existing predefined DQ-rate limit
	Source Category	Within / Above Threshold
	Source Reason/Finding	Existing explanation, when provided
Historical Analysis	Volume Status	Within / Above / Below historical expected range
	DQ Count Status	Historical statistical status
	DQ Rate Status	Historical statistical status
	Pattern	New / Persistent / Recurring / Resolved
Decision	Action	Investigate / Review / Monitor / No Action


The key is don't combine Source Category and our statistical DQ Rate Status into one status.

For example:

DQ Rate	Source Limit	Source Category	Historical Status	Pattern	Action

5.20%	3.00%	🔴 Above Threshold	🔴 Above Expected	New	🔴 Investigate
2.50%	3.00%	🟢 Within Threshold	🔴 Above Expected	New	🟠 Review
4.00%	3.00%	🔴 Above Threshold	🟢 Within Expected	Persistent	🟡 Monitor/Review
1.50%	3.00%	🟢 Within Threshold	🟢 Within Expected	—	🟢 No Action


The second and third examples are especially valuable. A UIM can still be within the official threshold but suddenly behave very differently from its history. Conversely, something can remain above its official threshold but be a longstanding known condition.

